{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 41, :comments ["<b>And you shall clothe them — Aaron your brother and his sons with him</b> — those designated by name in connection with them. This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Exodus 28:41). And the reason for \"with him\" is that on a single day he shall clothe them all and anoint them." "<b>And you shall anoint them and fill their hand</b> — every expression of filling of hands is an expression of consecration, when one enters to become established in it from that day onward. This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Exodus 28:41). But I do not understand his words — how the expression \"filling of hands\" can apply to the consecration of any thing. And as for what the Rav said, that in the foreign tongue, when a man is appointed to a command over something, the ruler gives a leather glove called a gant, and through that glove he installs him in the matter, and this is \"filling of hands\" — I do not know whether the Rav intends to say that the consecration is called \"filling of hands\" on account of the glove, and he brings proof from that custom. And know that this matter derived for them from the Torah itself, for among them it is the act of acquisition of the exchange of Boaz, and thus they translate \"a man drew off his shoe\" (Ruth 4:7) — that he draws off his gant, and they say that the redeemer gave it to Boaz, and this custom of theirs is mentioned in the books of their sages. Now we have spoken of this with proofs that are not decisive.\n\nBut the correct meaning of the expression \"filling of hands\" in the Torah is that it is an expression of completeness, as in \"for my days are fulfilled\" (Genesis 29:21 [sic]), \"and when these days are fulfilled\" (Esther 1:5), \"until the day of fulfillment\" (Leviticus 8:33) — when they are completed; and likewise \"full silver\" (Genesis 23:9) — a complete price. And the reason is this: the stranger, whose hand does not reach purification to offer a sacrifice or to perform the service of the kingship — behold, his hand is lacking in that service. But when he becomes qualified to do so, behold, his hand is complete — worthy for all services and labors. And likewise \"fill your hand today\" (Exodus 32:29) — for now your hand is full in all service of HaShem, \"for each man against his son and against his brother\" you served Him. Or perhaps it alludes to them that by virtue of this they will be chosen to serve the service of YHWH in the Tabernacle, as He said, \"at that time YHWH set apart the tribe of Levi\" (Deuteronomy 10:8).\n\nAnd King David said to Israel regarding the donation for the Temple: \"In my desire for the house of my Elohim I have a treasure — gold and silver I have given to the house of my Elohim,\" and he said to them: \"And who volunteers to fill his hand today to YHWH?\" (1 Chronicles 29:5) — for when they volunteer for the building of the House, their hand will be complete in all offerings and in all services, for the service of Israel to the Holy One, blessed be He, is not complete except in the Temple. And likewise the ram of filling in this section (Exodus 29:22), with which the filling of the priests is performed — for a sin offering and a burnt offering atone, but this is to fill his hand by offering it. And I found in the Jerusalem Targum that it translates \"the ram of filling\" — \"of completeness\" (Targum Jonathan on Exodus 29:22 and Leviticus 8:22), and so it translates in all of them with the meaning of completeness. But Onkelos (Onkelos on Exodus 28:41) pursues the sense and is not strict with the language, according to his custom in many places.\n\nAnd there are some who explain (Radak in the Book of Roots, root מלא) \"and you shall fill their hand\" — that he should fill their hand with the offerings, and likewise \"whoever desires, let him fill his hand\" (1 Kings 13:33) — that he should offer from them as much as he desires, the fill of his hands. But the correct interpretation is what I have explained. And the meaning of \"whoever desires, let him fill his hand and let him be priest of the high places\" (ibid.) is that he should bring filling-offerings for himself with a sacrifice as he invents from his own heart to consecrate himself, and afterward he shall be a priest of the high places, for they act in a manner resembling the ways of the Torah."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 28, :notes {1 "The verse cited as Genesis 29:21 is in fact Genesis 25:24 in the standard numbering; Ramban (or the textual tradition behind him) cites it as 29:21. Translated hyper-literally as the citation stands."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:19:06.122557996Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}